<section class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">LIQUIDNAT VS NETELASTIC</span><h1>A real comparison.<br><em>With clear boundaries.</em></h1><p class="lede">Both conversations cover a software broadband edge. The important differences are product maturity, integration scope, operator workflows and the evidence behind each proposed deployment.</p></section><section class="section"><div class="verdict-grid"><article><span class="eyebrow">LIQUIDNAT</span><h2>Explore the connected workflow.</h2><p>Gateway, RADIUS and Manager form the product direction. Today’s public demo lets you explore 12,864 fictional subscribers, custom speed profiles, CGNAT/direct/static changes, search and diagnosis. It does not forward live traffic.</p><a class="button" href="https://demo.liquidnat.com/">Open the interactive demo ↗</a></article><article><span class="eyebrow">NETELASTIC</span><h2>Evaluate a published product range.</h2><p>netElastic lists vBNG, CGNAT, a virtual router and netVision management. Its packages describe software licences and appliance options. Select an exact release and configuration for a commercial evaluation.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://netelastic.com/products" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official product range ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">FUNCTION BY FUNCTION</span><h2>Same network jobs.<br>Different evidence today.</h2><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="LiquidNAT and netElastic: implementation status and product scope"><table><caption>LiquidNAT and netElastic: implementation status and product scope</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">LiquidNAT status & experience</th><th scope="col">netElastic published scope / validation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">BNG access</th><td>Gateway architecture; production PPPoE/IPoE is not established by the demo.</td><td>Published vBNG packages list PPPoE and IPoE.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">IPv4 sharing</th><td>Simulated CGNAT admission and mapping history; live translation remains engine work.</td><td>CGNAT is offered separately and as a BNG option.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Identity</th><td>RADIUS product direction; demo auth rejection and recovery use fixtures.</td><td>Published BNG features list RADIUS AAA with CoA.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Routing</th><td>Independent forwarding foundations; FRRouting integration is architectural scope.</td><td>Virtual router product; BNG packages list dual-stack routing.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Management</th><td>Interactive Manager demo with reviewed changes and bounded subscriber search.</td><td>netVision is the published router management product.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Service changes</th><td>Create and assign simulated speed profiles; no real shaper or CoA is invoked.</td><td>Rate plans and QoS are listed in BNG features.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Addressing UX</th><td>Try CGNAT → static IPv4 without losing IPv6 context in the demo.</td><td>Ask for the same end-to-end operator workflow on the proposed version.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Diagnostics</th><td>Demo shows failed, blocked and unverified stages; selected evidence export is available.</td><td>Ask to demonstrate equivalent fault cases and export scope.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Availability</th><td>Simulated failover/upgrade jobs illustrate operations; no live continuity claim.</td><td>Request measured interruption and state survival for the selected topology.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Performance</th><td>No production capacity claim; interface graphs are synthetic.</td><td>Use a package-specific benchmark with intended services enabled.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Buying decision</th><td>Use the demo and scope a technical evaluation before committing to a rollout.</td><td>Obtain a release-, hardware-, capacity- and support-specific proposal.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="fine">Sources: <a href="https://netelastic.com/products" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">netElastic product overview</a> and <a href="https://netelastic.com/products/packages/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">package specifications</a>, reviewed 5 November 2026. Supplier listings are not independent benchmarks. Trademarks belong to their owners.</p></section><section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">THE SAME TEST FOR BOTH</span><h2>Four demonstrations. One evidence review.</h2><ol class="steps"><li><b>Connect a subscriber</b><p>Show the circuit, authentication, IPv4, delegated IPv6 prefix and installed policy together.</p></li><li><b>Change a service</b><p>Move from shared to static IPv4, then change speeds. Record reconnects, stale mappings and actual bidirectional policy.</p></li><li><b>Break a dependency</b><p>Reject authentication, exhaust a pool and withdraw an upstream route. Confirm the diagnostic result matches the actual fault.</p></li><li><b>Recover a node</b><p>Measure packet interruption, subscriber reconnection and existing NAT flow survival separately.</p></li></ol><p class="note"><strong>Then reconcile the evidence:</strong> fifth, compare the recorded outcomes, unresolved gaps and written commercial terms. Do not substitute screenshots or product-family claims for your acceptance test.</p></section><section class="editorial"><span class="eyebrow">MAKE THE SHORTLIST USEFUL</span><h2>Bring the topology.<br>We’ll work through the differences.</h2><p>Share subscriber mix, busy-hour traffic, IPv4 resources, access handoffs, RADIUS dependencies and recovery targets. A scoped comparison is more useful than a universal winner.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="contact.php?topic=Comparison">Discuss your comparison ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="compare.php#costs">Build a cost comparison →</a></div></section>
